t.card(s(1),'01','Identify two groups with conflicting interests where a profitable workshop’s waste damages neighbouring gardens.','Workshop owners gain private profit while neighbouring residents bear external damage.',['Feature/example (1): owners’ private gain. Distinct characteristic/example (1): neighbours’ external cost.'])
t.card(s(7),'01','Explain the error in equating any reed harvesting with unsustainable overexploitation when regeneration can support a monitored lower harvest.','Harvesting is resource exploitation or use. It becomes overexploitation when use exceeds sustainable limits or causes the relevant unsustainable damage; the lower managed option must be assessed from those conditions.',['Confusion (1): use equated with overuse. Boundary/test (1): regeneration and damage conditions distinguish them.'])
t.card(s(8),'01','Explain the error in reporting only N$5,000 external damage as social cost when the same activity also has N$8,000 private cost.','Social cost is private plus external cost: N$13,000. External damage is the third-party component, not the whole social cost.',['Confusion (1): component mistaken for whole. Boundary/test (1): N$8,000 + N$5,000 = N$13,000.'])
evals=[(2,'01-M01','Evaluate two feasible methods with private benefit N$12,000 each and no external benefits: original private cost N$8,000/external cost N$5,000; cleaner private cost N$9,000/external cost N$1,000.','Original private net benefit N$4,000 exceeds cleaner N$3,000, but original social net benefit −N$1,000 is below cleaner N$2,000. The cleaner method is supported on the stated social basis, conditional on full comparable effects and feasibility; who bears its cost still matters.'),(3,'01-M02','Evaluate a larger current reed harvest that raises this season’s earnings but is documented to damage regeneration and reduce future work; a lower managed harvest is feasible.','Current earnings support the larger harvest, but regeneration damage threatens future livelihoods and services. Prefer a managed level if it preserves future functions and allows feasible current livelihoods; the short-term gain alone is insufficient.'),(4,'01-M03','Evaluate a proposed ban against a monitored lower reed harvest where regeneration supports the lower level and households rely on some harvest income.','A ban conserves more of the resource but removes current livelihood output. Managed use can retain income and regeneration under the stated conditions. Prefer credible sustainable management if monitoring works; choose stricter conservation if damage persists.'),(5,'01-M04','Evaluate a temporary basket tariff with credible efficiency training and a review date, where local producers gain room to adjust but buyers face higher prices.','Protection can support producers and adjustment during training, but higher buyer costs and weaker competition are limitations. Conditional time-limited support is defensible if improvement is feasible and review removes ineffective protection; permanent support is not established.'),(6,'01-M05','Evaluate tax-funded public access-path spending where several producers will use it regularly, maintenance is funded, but taxes reduce private purchases and another useful project is forgone.','Shared access and lower delivery barriers support the path. Tax withdrawal and the displaced project are opportunity costs. Support requires the path’s useful shared gains to exceed the feasible alternatives, with distribution and maintenance considered; public ownership alone does not decide.')]
for n,l,p,a in evals:t.card(s(n),l,p,a,['Argument for (2): relevant gain (1), developed consequence or numerical evidence (1). Argument against (2): opposing cost/interest (1), developed consequence (1). Condition (1): decisive feasibility, time or management requirement. Judgement (2): conditional choice (1), linked to the actual comparison (1). Total 7.'])
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][0],'01-M01 01-M02 01-M03 01-M05','Identify private and external costs where a workshop pays wages and its dust damages neighbours’ gardens (2). Identify short- and long-term effects where intensive harvest raises current earnings but damages future regeneration (2). Explain the conservation conflict for households relying on harvest income (2). Identify expenditure types for a government-funded shared path and a household’s own basket purchase (2).','Wages are private cost; garden damage external cost. Current earnings are short-term and later regeneration damage long-term. Conservation preserves future resources but can reduce current livelihood output. The path is public spending and the household purchase private spending.',['Identification, separate points (2): wages private (1), gardens external (1). Time effects (2): current earnings (1), future damage (1). Explanation (2): future resource benefit (1), current income sacrifice (1). Spending (2): public path (1), private purchase (1). Marks allocation 8.'])
