t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][1],'01-M01 01-M02 01-M03 01-M04 01-M05',base+' Describe three private/social/time features with case details (6). Explain the social-cost calculation and the conflict between current and future effects (7). Discuss managed harvest, protection and path spending together, weighing the affected interests (7).','Private cost is N$8,000, external damage N$5,000 and current earnings contrast with future regeneration loss. Social cost N$13,000 exceeds benefit N$12,000, so private net gain N$4,000 coexists with social net loss N$1,000. Intensive use supports present income but weakens future resources. Managed harvest can retain livelihoods; temporary protection needs credible improvement because buyers pay; the path needs gains exceeding forgone alternatives. Conditional targeted measures are stronger than unrestricted support.',['Data description (6): private expense (1), N$8,000 detail (1); external damage (1), N$5,000 detail (1); time conflict (1), current income/future regeneration detail (1). Mechanism/explanation (7): social cost sum (1), N$13,000 (1), social net −N$1,000 (1), private gain N$4,000 (1); external omission changes judgement (1), current income benefit (1), later resource loss (1). Both sides/judgement (7): managed livelihood benefit (1), conservation/monitoring constraint (1); protection adjustment benefit (1), buyer-cost/review limitation (1); path shared benefit versus private/other-project opportunity cost (1); condition credible sustainable affordable implementation (1); judgement targeted conditional support linked to all three choices (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'],[base])
cases='Invented resource communities. Case 1: current harvesting provides private jobs/income; a monitored level preserves regeneration. A temporary tariff has credible efficiency training/review but raises consumer prices. A regularly used public access path has funded maintenance but requires taxes that reduce private spending. Case 2: extraction causes irreversible long-term resource damage; producer protection lacks an improvement plan while imposing high costs on buyers; a proposed public path is rarely used and would displace valuable water provision. Free trade offers cheaper goods in both cases but exposes current producers to adjustment.'
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][2],'01-M03 01-M04 01-M05',cases+' Evaluate managed use, temporary protection and public-path spending in Case 1 (7). Evaluate the proposed package in Case 2, including conservation, trade interests and the alternative use of public funds (7).','Case 1: sustainable harvesting retains income, temporary protection may support credible adjustment and a useful path can serve several users. Consumer and taxpayer costs remain. Conditional support requires monitoring, review and gains exceeding private alternatives. Case 2: irreversible damage favours conservation; costly protection without improvement weakens the case against cheaper trade with adjustment support. Rarely used path spending is poorly supported where valuable water provision is displaced. Reject the package as described, conditional on those facts rather than a blanket opposition to resource use or public spending.',['Case 1 marks (7): argument for livelihood/sustainable use (1), credible producer adjustment (1), useful public access (1); opposing resource risk if monitoring fails (1), consumer/taxpayer opportunity costs (1); condition effective monitoring/review and net value (1); judgement conditional package support linked to three choices (1). Case 2 allocation (7): argument for current producer income/jobs (1), possible shared-access aim (1); argument against irreversible resource loss (1), costly ineffective protection (1), displaced water provision (1); condition supplied long-term damage and weak plans (1); judgement reject package with conservation/open-trade adjustment/valuable-spending alternative (1). Total 14.'],[cases])
